# IPC contract

Renderer access uses only `window.flowpilot`; Node, Electron, filesystem, and
SQLite are not exposed. The API is defined by `FlowPilotApi` in
`flowpilot-api.ts`, with `Result<T, ErrorCode>` and `ErrorCode` in
`ipc-result.ts`.

- `snapshot.load()` (`snapshot.load`): empty strict request; returns
  an `IpcSnapshot`.
- `commands.apply()` (`commands.apply`): strict
  `{ commands, expectedRevision }`; returns the updated `IpcSnapshot`.
- `settings.get()` (`settings.get`): empty strict request; returns settings.
- `settings.set()` (`settings.set`): strict
  `{ key, value, expectedRevision }`; returns the updated `IpcSnapshot`.
- `app.info()` (`app.info`): empty strict request; returns `{ name, version, now }`,
  where `now` is the ISO timestamp from the main-process clock.
- `catalog.list()` (`catalog.list`): empty strict request; returns task types and
  disciplines. Catalog mutations use the validated `commands.apply()` channel.
- `rules.list()` (`rules.list`): empty strict request; returns rule documents,
  status, validation errors, diagnostics, and accepted versions.
- `rules.importFile()` (`rules.importFile`): empty strict request; main opens a
  JSON file dialog, checks the 1 MiB limit before reading, validates the file,
  and returns a draft or path-specific `RuleInvalid` errors.
- `rules.exportFile({ id })` (`rules.exportFile`): main opens a save dialog and
  writes only the accepted rule version. `rules.activate`, `rules.deactivate`,
  and `rules.bind` accept strict rule-id/scope requests and return the updated
  rule. Cancellation and activation conflicts are explicit result errors.

Every reply is `{ ok: true, value }` or `{ ok: false, error }`. Error codes
include `StaleRevision`, `ValidationFailed`, `UndoConflict`, `NotFound`,
`AlreadyUndone`, `NotUndoable`, `FileNotFound`, `TooLarge`, `AlreadyExists`,
`NotReadable`, `MissingFile`, `PackageInvalid`, `Cancelled`, and `InternalError`.
The rule API (`catalog.list` and every `rules.*` method) uses `IpcRuleResult<T>`
instead: failures have an `error` object with `kind` equal to `ValidationFailed`,
`Cancelled`, `NotFound`, `RuleConflict`, or `InternalError`, or
`{ kind: 'RuleInvalid', errors }`, where each error has `path`, `code`, `reason`,
and `suggestion` strings. Schemas reject unknown fields. Settings writes use the same expected-revision
transaction as commands. Filesystem paths do not cross the bridge except the
display-only export folder returned by `backup.export()`; renderer requests do
not accept paths. Main resolves `<profile>/flowpilot.db` and owns the single
writer. `IpcSnapshot` carries dates and times as ISO strings for safe IPC
serialization.
AI proposals use a separate strict command contract in `ai-commands.ts`.
`task.classify`, `rule.import`, `rule.activate`, `rule.deactivate`, and
`rule.bind` are internal main-to-storage commands and are intentionally absent
from `ipcCommandSchema`. The proposal validator checks authoritative catalog and
active-rule snapshots plus revision/rule fingerprints before returning commands
tagged with `source: 'ai'`; it does not call a provider or apply writes itself.
The provider boundary checks task, nested, column, catalog, and rule references
against authoritative request data.
AI `task.update` accepts only user-editable fields. AI `task.create` uses a
separate strict input shape; conversion derives status from the target column,
assigns the next add order, and initializes comments and attachments without
accepting system fields from the proposal.
Task fields may enter the model only with a migration, storage write, read path,
command schema, and invariant test in the same change.

AI calls are explicit through `ai.run()` and `ai.cancel()`. The main-process
`ai.run()` boundary serializes Temporal date and time values to canonical ISO
strings before returning a proposal to the renderer. The manual provider also
exposes `ai.prompt()` and `ai.reply()` for text exchange without clipboard or
network access. Replies pass strict proposal and command validation before they
leave the provider. The fake provider is selected only in unpackaged
`FLOWPILOT_TEST=1` builds; manual exchange is the default. No persisted setting
selects an AI provider.

The main process builds a private `referenceIndex` from its repository snapshot,
separate from the limited model-visible context. It contains task/column IDs
and states and is never accepted from or included in the renderer/model request.
This lets validation detect collisions with completed, over-limit, and nested
records without exposing those records to the model. Move/create validation also
enforces the #44 column/status semantics. Task-type, discipline, and rule
catalogs are persisted by the storage repository and remain main-process-only;
the provider boundary rejects unknown catalog references. `task.move.status.until`
is serialized to an ISO date string at the IPC boundary.

`FLOWPILOT_TEST_AI_REPLY` may provide a JSON-encoded proposal in an unpackaged
test build with `FLOWPILOT_TEST=1`; the startup fake returns it through
`ai.run`. Missing or invalid test replies return `ProviderUnavailable`.

Test variables `FLOWPILOT_TEST`, `FLOWPILOT_TEST_NOW`, `FLOWPILOT_TEST_PROFILE`,
and `FLOWPILOT_TEST_AI_REPLY` are honored only for unpackaged applications with
`FLOWPILOT_TEST=1`. Packaged apps always use system time, Electron's user-data
directory, and manual exchange for AI. The injected `Clock` makes tests deterministic.
History is available through `history.list(limit?, before?)` and
`history.undo(entryId?)`. A missing `entryId` undoes the newest compatible entry.
List requests are strict and require a positive integer limit.

`attachments.add({ taskId })` opens the main-process file dialog; renderer paths
are rejected. `attachments.open({ attachmentId })` resolves only a path from the
repository, and `attachments.remove({ attachmentId })` records the change through
the repository transaction.

`backup.export()` opens a folder dialog and returns the selected export folder
and its ISO export timestamp for display only; its request is empty and strict.
`backup.verify()` opens a package-folder dialog and returns validation problems
or an opaque package token. Only that token is accepted by
`backup.restore(packageToken)`. Package paths and contents remain in main;
restore closes and reopens the repository around the storage restore operation.
